LLM coding agents now resolve most issues in benchmarks such as SWE-bench Verified, so the bottleneck moves to judging their patches. Reviewers and change impact analysis judge an edit by its distance from where a human would fix the defect, while test-based acceptance keeps every edit of a passing patch. Neither tells a reviewer which edits the intended outcome actually depends on. We present \tool{}, an outcome-relative edit triage. \tool{} states the intent as goal and preservation test obligations, splits a patch into units linked by a requires relation, and uses counterfactual leave-one-out and closure-aware delta debugging to label every unit as necessary, jointly needed, coupled or unneeded, with soundness and closed 1-minimality guarantees relative to the test oracle. A dependency-selected envelope attributes behaviour changes outside the obligations to units. A footprint study of \rqoneSystems{} LLMs in one agent scaffold shows that agent patches are not larger or more scattered than human patches. Executing \nValidPatches{} resolved agent and reference patches for Django and SymPy, we find that \rqtwoAgentSrcUnneeded{} of agent source edits and \rqtwoGoldSrcUnneeded{} of reference source edits are not needed for the obligations. Location proxies recover necessity only partly (MCC \rqtwoMccPgoldfile{}), because \rqtwoUnneededInGoldShare{} of removable edits lie inside the reference files. Five open-weight LLM judges reach MCC of at most \rqtwoBestLlmMcc{} (\rqtwoInfMaxMcc{} when shown the tests), with low recall. The envelope raises the detection of injected faults from \rqthreeSemOffShort{} to \rqthreeSemEnvShort{}, and triage costs a median of \rqfourCallsMedian{} test runs per patch.
